<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  earleybird local suite overrides
  =================================

  The official iXML conformance suite (symlinked at ./ixml) is treated as
  READ-ONLY. This file lets earleybird adjust how a *named* catalog test case is
  judged, in version-controlled, earleybird-owned data, without editing upstream.

  It is general-purpose, not ambiguity-specific. Each <override> targets a test
  by its full catalog name and either:

    action="replace"  - replace the catalog's expected results entirely
    action="augment"  - append extra acceptable results to the catalog's list

  Child assertions may be any of:

    <assert-ambiguous/>            local-only: pass iff the input is accepted as a
                                   sentence AND the serialization is flagged
                                   ixml:state="ambiguous". The parse-tree SHAPE is
                                   not checked.
    <assert-xml> ... </assert-xml> an additional acceptable serialization
    <assert-not-a-sentence/>       the input should be rejected
    <assert-not-a-grammar/>        the grammar source should fail to compile
    <assert-dynamic-error code=""/> a dynamic (D-series) error is expected

  GUIDANCE: do NOT paste one processor's arbitrary tree into the answer key to
  force a pass — that overfits the oracle to earleybird's current, incidental
  tree-selection order and breaks again the moment that order changes. For
  ambiguous cases prefer `replace` + <assert-ambiguous/>, which checks exactly
  what the iXML spec requires (a valid tree exists + it is flagged ambiguous) and
  is robust to internal changes.
-->
<suite-overrides>

  <!--
    misc/sample.grammar.12/g12.c05
    Grammar:  S: A+.  A: (A, A)+; "a"+.
    Input:    aaaaaaaa  (eight 'a's)

    This grammar is deliberately hyper-ambiguous: "one or more a's" has hundreds
    of structurally distinct parse trees for 8 a's (S:A+ compositions x A:"a"+
    flat runs x A:(A,A)+ Catalan bracketings). The catalog can only enumerate a
    SAMPLE of acceptable trees, and its list is explicitly truncated in-source
    with ellipsis comment markers. earleybird returns a valid tree (a 7+1 flat split,
    <A>aaaaaaa</A><A>a</A>), correctly flagged ambiguous, that simply is not one
    of the enumerated samples — so exact-match reports a spurious FAIL.

    The iXML spec leaves the choice of tree among ambiguous parses undefined
    (Steven Pemberton, "Document on Ambiguity", public-ixml 2026-01-20: "it is
    not reasonable to require a particular parse of the ambiguous input, so it is
    unspecified which parse is serialised"). The conformance-correct check here is
    therefore: accepted-as-a-sentence AND flagged ambiguous. Full analysis in
    log/g12.c05-explained.md.
  -->
  <override test="misc/sample.grammar.12/g12.c05" action="replace"
            reason="Hyper-ambiguous grammar; valid-tree set too large for the catalog to enumerate (answer list truncated upstream). Spec leaves tree choice undefined; conformance = accepted + flagged ambiguous. See log/g12.c05-explained.md.">
    <assert-ambiguous/>
  </override>

</suite-overrides>
